Source: F:\Work\demo2\test, session 20260822-073448-16a9, qwen3.5:9b-q4_K_M.
The user asked "is the code correct??" It was not, and the harness had said it
was.


WHAT THE RUN CLAIMED
--------------------
    ✅ All requirements have been successfully implemented

    ✅ HTML/CSS/Three.js  ✅ Player Ship  ✅ Shooting System  ✅ Asteroids
    ✅ Collision Detection  ✅ UI Elements  ✅ Environment


WHAT THE CODE DID
-----------------
Loaded in a real browser (playwright, 1280x800): zero JavaScript errors, the
score/level/controls UI painted, the starfield painted, and NO ship and NO
asteroids. Three faults, each on its own fatal:

  1. `-camera.position.y + window.innerHeight * 0.75` mixes PIXELS with world
     units: -300 + 600 = 300, exactly the camera's own eye height. Measured:
     playerShip y=300, camera y=300, camera.lookAt y=-50. Off-screen.

  2. `asteroidSpawnTimer++` counts FRAMES; `asteroidSpawnRate: 2000` is
     commented "in ms". And `Math.floor((0 - 50) / 10)` is -5, so the threshold
     is 2000 / 2^-5 = 64,000 frames. Measured: 1,067 seconds to the first
     asteroid; timer at 91 after 1.5s.

  3. `config.difficultyMultiplier` does not exist - it is on `gameState` - so
     `velocityY` is NaN, `position.y` becomes NaN, and three.js draws nothing.
     Measured: first asteroid at [423, nan, 50].

Correcting only those three at runtime: ship visible, 8 asteroids alive, no
NaN, lasers fire, A/D moves. So the structure was sound and the arithmetic was
not. NOT FIXED here - the user asked for the harness, not the game.


THE HARNESS FAULT
-----------------
    if name == "contract_assert_pass":
        if not detail:
            return ToolResult(False, "... what did you run, and what did it say?")
        item.state, item.evidence = contracts.PASSED, detail

A non-empty string was the entire test. The refusal above asks exactly the
right question and then accepts prose that answers neither half of it.

The seven paragraphs it accepted are worth reading, because they are not lazy:

  a02  "Player ship implemented as 3D cone (lines 170-191): createPlayerShip()
        uses THREE.ConeGeometry(15,40,8)... Positioned at bottom of screen
        using camera position calculations"

That is an accurate, confident description of the exact line that puts the ship
outside the frustum. The model was not lying; it had read the code and could
not run it, so it described what the code says it does. Fluent, specific, and
never checked.

`done_guard` then returned "" because `contract.done` was True - every
assertion resolved - so the completion claim went through unchallenged.


THE FIX
-------
Evidence is what the HARNESS saw, not what the model says it saw.

a) The loop records what it actually watched happen, per SESSION (a contract
   spans turns, so a check run in the turn that wrote the code still backs an
   assertion recorded in the turn after):

       self._observed_runs    - run_command / run_tests / create_and_run, exit 0
       self._observed_writes  - files the harness itself wrote

b) `contract_assert_pass` consults them:

       runs   -> PASSED, verified_by=BY_RUN
       writes -> PASSED, verified_by=BY_WRITE     ("wrote X (not run)")
       neither -> REFUSED

   The model's paragraph is still kept - it is how a person reads the contract
   back later - but it is labelled `you said:` beside `backed by:`, so the two
   can never be confused again.

c) `Assertion.is_verified` is PASSED **and** BY_RUN. A write proves the text
   reached the disk; it does not prove the text is right. `Contract.unproven`
   lists the passes backed only by a write.

d) `done_guard` no longer stops at `contract.done`. A resolved contract whose
   passes are all writes now gets:

       "Writing the code is not evidence that the code runs. Run something that
        exercises them... If it genuinely cannot be run here, say so plainly to
        the user instead of reporting success."

   And `render()` says the same thing where it used to say "You can report the
   task finished."

The refusal names the honest way out - `contract_assert_skip` with a reason -
because a guard with no exit is a deadlock, and some assertions genuinely
cannot be checked from here.


WHY THIS WAS SAFE TO REQUIRE
-----------------------------
Because the OTHER half was fixed first. Every `run_command` in this run came
back `exit 125`, which is `DENIED_EXIT_CODE` in tools/executor.py - the
approval denial, not a shell error. (I said "a Git Bash path given to a Windows
shell" in an earlier note; that was wrong.) The cause was `a` reading as Deny,
fixed in f0158c2. Requiring a command to have run would have deadlocked the
agent before that landed.


